Syscall User Dispatch (SUD) for FreeBSD/amd64

Allows a Wine process to register an allow-range VA window plus a
userspace selector byte so that SYSCALL instructions whose RIP falls
outside the allow-range are redirected as SIGSYS rather than executed.
Wine registers its __wine_syscall_dispatcher thunk as the allow-range;
Windows PE stubs that issue SYSCALL directly are intercepted and handled
by Wine's signal handler, which translates the Windows syscall number
and emulates the call. Equivalent to Linux PR_SET_SYSCALL_USER_DISPATCH
(Linux 5.11).

Design
------
- Per-thread struct sud_state is heap-allocated and hung off td->td_sud.
  The asm hot-path does a single null-pointer test; no TDP_/TDP2_ flag
  bit is consumed.  (All TDP_ bits 0x1..0x80000000 are currently used.)
- td_sud is placed after td_ktr_io_lim, past the td_endcopy boundary,
  so fork1()'s bcopy(td_startcopy..td_endcopy) does NOT copy it.
  It is explicitly initialized to NULL in fork1() instead.
- KBI: new field is appended after td_ktr_io_lim, past all four offsets
  asserted by _Static_assert in kern_thread.c (td_flags@0x108,
  td_pflags@0x114, td_frame@0x4e8, td_emuldata@0x6f0).  No assert change.
- Selector is read with fubyte(9) in the C helper.  fubyte returns -1 on
  page fault; we treat that as ALLOW (fail-open) so a bad selector pointer
  does not permanently wedge the process.
- Interrupts: fast_syscall_common already emits `sti` before our insertion
  point (after handle_ibrs_entry, before saving the remaining GPRs).
  Interrupts are therefore already enabled when the SUD hot-path runs.
  The `sti` inside the SUD block is defensive documentation; it is a
  no-op in the current code but makes the liveness of IF explicit to
  future readers.  On the BLOCK path we jump to label 1: where the
  existing `cli` re-disables interrupts before the PCB_FULL_IRET test.
- SIGSYS is queued via trapsignal(9).  Delivery goes through the existing
  AST path on sysretq exit (label 1: / userret() loop in
  fast_syscall_common).  The helper must NOT call userret() itself.
- SUD is self-only, matching Linux PR_SET_SYSCALL_USER_DISPATCH: PROC_SUD_SET
  and PROC_SUD_CLEAR operate only on the calling thread (td == curthread).
  This eliminates the cross-thread UAF race that would exist if td_sud were
  written for a thread that is concurrently executing amd64_syscall_sud_check:
  the hot-path loads td->td_sud without any lock, so a concurrent free() from
  another thread would be a use-after-free.  With self-only semantics td_sud
  is only ever written by td itself; since td is currently executing procctl(2)
  it cannot simultaneously be in the hot-path.  Wine always calls
  procctl(P_PID, getpid(), PROC_SUD_SET, ...) from the thread that needs
  interception, matching this model exactly.
  Both callbacks enforce self-only at the API boundary: if the procctl
  framework resolves a target process other than td->td_proc the call is
  rejected with EPERM.
- fork/clone inheritance: SUD state is intentionally not inherited across
  fork(2) or thread creation, matching the behavior of Linux
  PR_SET_SYSCALL_USER_DISPATCH. Child processes and threads always start
  with SUD disabled (td_sud = NULL) to prevent cross-thread memory sharing 
  of the userspace selector pointer.
- On exec(2): SUD is cleared in exec_onexec_old(), alongside
  sigfastblock_clear().  exec_new_vmspace() sets vmspace_destroyed=true
  before calling exec_onexec_old(), so the old VA range and selector
  pointer are already gone when we free the state.
- kern_thr.c: newtd->td_sud = NULL is set BEFORE initialize_thread() so
  that the thread_free() error path in thread_create() is always safe.
  td_sud lies past both td_endzero and td_endcopy; neither the bzero
  nor bcopy in thread_create() touches it.  The UMA zone has no
  UMA_ZONE_ZERO and thread_init/thread_ctor do not initialise td_sud.
- MALLOC_DEFINE(M_SUD) lives in trap.c (where amd64_syscall_sud_check
  lives).  All other TUs use MALLOC_DECLARE via sud.h.
- trap.c already uses fubyte() (lines 635-642) so vm/vm_extern.h is
  already pulled in; only <sys/malloc.h> and <sys/sud.h> are new.
- The SIGSYS si_code is SIGSYS_DISPATCH=1.  The SYS_ prefix is avoided
  because FreeBSD's <sys/syscall.h> already uses SYS_ for syscall numbers
  (SYS_exit=1 etc.); a SYS_-prefixed si_code in <sys/signal.h> would sit in
  the same namespace and cause confusion.  Linux uses SYS_USER_DISPATCH=2 with
  the same semantic; SIGSYS_DISPATCH follows FreeBSD's per-signal prefix
  convention (ILL_, FPE_, SEGV_, BUS_, ...) instead.
  SIGSYS_DISPATCH=1 is placed after the SIGPOLL codes block, before the
  closing #endif, so it sits in its own clearly labelled section and does
  not appear visually nested inside the SIGILL block.
- 32-bit compat (freebsd32): not implemented.  Wine on amd64 is 64-bit;
  a freebsd32 thunk for proc_sud_ctl is left as future work.

Files changed
-------------
  sys/sys/sud.h                  NEW
  sys/sys/signal.h               add SIGSYS_DISPATCH si_code
  sys/sys/proc.h                 add td_sud to struct thread
  sys/sys/procctl.h              add PROC_SUD_SET / PROC_SUD_CLEAR
  sys/amd64/amd64/genassym.c     add TD_SUD, SUD_START/LEN/SELECTOR
  sys/amd64/amd64/exception.S    hot-path check in fast_syscall_common
  sys/amd64/amd64/trap.c         MALLOC_DEFINE(M_SUD) + amd64_syscall_sud_check()
  sys/kern/kern_thread.c         free td_sud in thread_free_batched();
                                 add thread_sud_clear()
  sys/kern/kern_fork.c           initialize td_sud to NULL after bcopy
  sys/kern/kern_procctl.c        table entries + sud_set() + sud_clear();
                                 add proc_sud_ctl to sys_procctl data union
  sys/kern/kern_exec.c           clear td_sud in exec_onexec_old()
  sys/kern/kern_thr.c            initialize td_sud to NULL in thread_create()
  sys/amd64/amd64/exec_machdep.c clear the selector on signal delivery

